WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.389 --> 00:00:06.389
Volumetric Fog is an optional part of the Exponential Height Fog actor.

2
00:00:06.389 --> 00:00:18.710
Volumetric Fog computes participating media density and lighting at every point in the camera frustum to support varying densities and any number of lights that affect the fog.

3
00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:27.710
When working with Volumetric Fog, it's important to keep in mind that while you can increase the visual fidelity of the fog, you should be mindful of the associated performance costs.

4
00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:38.100
In some situations, the trade-off for higher visual quality is at the cost of performance, such as LED walls used in virtual production.

5
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:43.100
In just a second, we'll create a Volumetric Fog actor and adjust its properties.

6
00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:50.100
But first I want to make note of how you can individually manipulate other actors, such as spotlights, point lights, etc.

7
00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:53.100
Their contributions to the Volumetric Fog.

8
00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:57.189
Typically, you'll have a parameter called Volumetric Scattering Intensity.

9
00:00:57.189 --> 00:01:05.189
While the default value of 1 is usually sufficient, there are situations where you may want more or less of the effect in your scene.

10
00:01:05.189 --> 00:01:08.189
Simply adjust this value as you see fit.

11
00:01:08.189 --> 00:01:18.959
Let's jump over to our project, and within our Content Browser, in our Maps folder, we're going to open up the Lit Atmos in class.

12
00:01:18.959 --> 00:01:26.959
So if I switch to Lit mode, you'll see that we have a few of our actors emitting light, but generally speaking, the rest of the scene is unlit.

13
00:01:26.959 --> 00:01:28.959
And that's what we want to start with.

14
00:01:28.959 --> 00:01:36.959
So before we add our Exponential Height Fog actor and enable our Volumetric Fog, we need to add some lights to our scene.

15
00:01:36.959 --> 00:01:42.469
I'm going to go up to Window and open our Environment Light Mixer.

16
00:01:42.469 --> 00:01:50.469
Now this is going to be just a really fast way to add all of our core components that would make up our dynamic real-time lighting.

17
00:01:50.469 --> 00:01:56.469
And that's going to be our Skylight, Directional Light, Sky Atmosphere, Volumetric Clouds is optional.

18
00:01:56.469 --> 00:01:58.469
Of course, we're also going to want our Height Fog.

19
00:01:58.469 --> 00:02:05.599
So I'm just going to simply click through each of these, and you'll see that it adds the component to our scene.

20
00:02:05.599 --> 00:02:11.599
Now this is obviously a little blown out, and we'll make just a couple adjustments.

21
00:02:11.599 --> 00:02:20.599
Now if I go over to our Outliner, you'll see that here we have our Directional Light, Exponential Height Fog, Sky Atmosphere, Skylight, and Volumetric Clouds.

22
00:02:20.599 --> 00:02:28.699
Now if you want to, I'm just going to select these actors and simply create them in the folder.

23
00:02:28.699 --> 00:02:33.180
Let's call this Environment. There we go.

24
00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:39.180
Now we can go back to our Environment Light Mixer, and you'll see that we have Minimal, Normal, Normal, and Advanced.

25
00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:43.180
All this is going to do is just show different settings.

26
00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:47.180
So Minimal is the typical settings that we will adjust.

27
00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:52.270
Normal shows most of the other parameters that we have here.

28
00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:55.270
And then Normal and Advanced is just going to expand out a few more.

29
00:02:55.270 --> 00:03:01.270
So for this case, we'll just keep it at Minimal. We're not looking for anything super fancy.

30
00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:08.039
In the next video, we'll walk through how to adjust our Volumetric Fog and change the visual properties of it.
